on/998bbb73089f4f525a1052cfc88507994fcfa7d945725dd7d6f984911ecf4f6c" "q-140986/selection/0891c8b5d5b1b7cdaaaa41315bf9e8c0e4a9a23fd1f44109728df1b8919ff2b1" "q-140986/selection/fe10750ab2dde2b829bacbf3b5b89e7f36c900e96da07c436dcd038261fc416f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140986" :question/id #uuid "c82f5129-4cd7-5326-9dc3-84b2ccee4d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140986" :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+40{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+40{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}))-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+12{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-36{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group ${x}_{2}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{2},$ factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{2}^{2},$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}]-4{x}_{3}^{2})+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140986/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e5e149-54cd-528e-b50e-1cafecb7b77a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140986"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8184"] :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2x_3+x_3^2$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/d041c39fe285ee5fdbd8022b70a8043a4ff07cbeb1dae8c49905248df1b86d01.png)\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_3^2-2x_1^2$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where $i \\neq j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3.$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8185"] :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/cc77cb76fcab6bd72211e7e66d5fee5ba87c0ba1465571aa88baf26beb196a22.png)\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/e1048614ed3dda333597bf32339f23a1585308e630233e3d11751c48c597ed52.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2 .$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8186"] :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/bf0297b0d758d02198d01565050bc5f3b02b0964336d3b84fdca331fabed6051.png)\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}))+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2,$ factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2,$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+9{x}_{3}^{2}]-9{x}_{3}^{2})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+9{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/84f88cbb41e573d7ed0793919e0ad25477a46637d09144ca9f462e12f0bc6e90.png)\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
